      "type": "text",
      "text": "{\"unit_id\": \"Minemaes\", \"choices\": [\"C1\"], \"reasoning\": \"C1 Mnw-ms.w is the god Min (Mnw) plus ms.w, 'born', translated Min-mesu. Its consonants are m-n-(w)-m-s-(w). Minemaes gives m-n-m-s. The weak w after Mnw and the plural ending .w may go unwritten, and the vowels e and a are conventional fillers that stand for no sign. Min- renders Mnw and -maes renders ms(.w), with no extra or missing consonant. Review Y reads the name as Min-em-aes, with a preposition m and a further word 'aes'. The records name no such word. Any ꜣs or ꜥs would reduce to s anyway, which leaves the same skeleton m-n-m-s. With no different word identifiable, the reading is Min + ms. That D&H usually write ms as -mose is a habit, not a rule; they also write Ramesses with -mes-. So record 1 reads as exactly Min-mesu: same name. C2 Jmn-ms.w is the god Amun (Jmn) plus ms.w, translated Amun-mesu. Dropping the weak j would let the consonants fit, but Min- renders the god Min (Mnw), not Amun, which would be rendered Amen- or Amun-. Different words, so C2 is a different name.\"}"
